 "tts": "Musk's hint at a five-hundred-ton Raptor 4 could unlock greater deep-space capability for Starship.\n\nThis is Space X Daily, episode one hundred fifty-four.\n\nHeat-shield data from the latest orbital reentry, jet generators in Mississippi, then the Counterpoint and an engineering deep dive.\n\nRaptor 4 development, Business Upturn reports, is meant to raise thrust for lunar and interplanetary trajectories after the Super Heavy booster separates. The work scales that thrust while keeping the reusability features already shown on earlier Raptor versions.\n\nStarship's upper stage now carries, in a MiGFlug analysis, the first extended dataset on how individual heat-shield tiles behaved through a full reentry from orbital speed on Flight thirteen. The review tracks those tiles under peak heating, any ablation — material wearing away — and whether the attachments held after the Ship returned.\n\nex A I has installed eight refurbished jet-engine generators at its Mississippi facility, Mezha reports, to cover power for growing compute infrastructure. The machines supply immediate capacity while permanent grid connections are finished, and the install is part of expanding Colossus-class systems, the large training clusters tied to the broader Musk ecosystem.",
 "reader": "Musk's hint at a five-hundred-ton Raptor 4 could unlock greater deep-space capability for Starship.\n\nThis is SpaceX Daily, episode 154.\n\nHeat-shield data from the latest orbital reentry, jet generators in Mississippi, then the Counterpoint and an engineering deep dive.\n\nRaptor 4 development, Business Upturn reports, is meant to raise thrust for lunar and interplanetary trajectories after the Super Heavy booster separates. The work scales that thrust while keeping the reusability features already shown on earlier Raptor versions.\n\nStarship's upper stage now carries, in a MiGFlug analysis, the first extended dataset on how individual heat-shield tiles behaved through a full reentry from orbital speed on Flight thirteen. The review tracks those tiles under peak heating, any ablation — material wearing away — and whether the attachments held after the Ship returned.\n\nxAI has installed eight refurbished jet-engine generators at its Mississippi facility, Mezha reports, to cover power for growing compute infrastructure. The machines supply immediate capacity while permanent grid connections are finished, and the install is part of expanding Colossus-class systems, the large training clusters tied to the broader Musk ecosystem.",
